<article class="env-definition" id="0FKM">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FKM">Definition <span data-tag="0FKM">17.30.1</span>.</a> In the situation above, the <em>de Rham complex of $\mathcal{B}$ over $\mathcal{A}$</em> is the unique complex </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \Omega _{\mathcal{B}/\mathcal{A}}^0 \to \Omega _{\mathcal{B}/\mathcal{A}}^1 \to \Omega _{\mathcal{B}/\mathcal{A}}^2 \to \ldots  \]
</div>
  <p> of sheaves of $\mathcal{A}$-modules whose differential in degree $0$ is given by $\text{d} : \mathcal{B} \to \Omega _{\mathcal{B}/\mathcal{A}}$ and whose differentials in higher degrees have the following property </p>
  <div class="equation" id="0FKN">
    <span class="equation-label"><a href="/tag/0FKN" data-tag="0FKN">17.30.1.1</a></span>
  <div>\begin{equation}  \label{modules-equation-rule} \text{d}\left(b_0\text{d}b_1 \wedge \ldots \wedge \text{d}b_ p\right) = \text{d}b_0 \wedge \text{d}b_1 \wedge \ldots \wedge \text{d}b_ p \end{equation}</div>
</div>
  <p> where $b_0, \ldots , b_ p \in \mathcal{B}(U)$ are sections over a common open $U \subset X$. </p>
</article>